        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains severely constricted by the ongoing crisis. The most consequential unverified development involves allegations that Iran struck a UAE ADNOC tanker with missiles in the southern corridor. Ibtimes.co.in carried the strike claim; sana.sy reported Yemen condemning an attack on a UAE oil tanker; and investinglive.com cited reports of Iranian missiles hitting a tanker off Oman in the southern corridor. None of these constitutes independent confirmation of the other, and no independent verification is available. Separately, per globalsecurity.org (unverified, single-source), the Arab League Secretary-General condemned the targeting of a UAE vessel and held Iran responsible for escalation. On the diplomatic side, Iran has publicly articulated conditions for reopening the strait, with khaama.com and multiple other unverified single-source outlets reporting Tehran will not act without its demands being met by the United States; no agreement between Iran and the United States has been confirmed by either party. The oil price feed has been dark for over two hours; no Brent or WTI print is available at the time of writing. PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-08-02, recorded 2 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day. Separately, port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports totalled 502 vessels in the most recent 24 hours, a figure using a different methodology and not comparable to the PortWatch transit count. The Crisis Pressure index sits in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast is in the elevated band and rising over 24 hours, though it carries a low-confidence flag due to thin contract volume, with Polymarket escalation contracts as the top contributor. Those two readings diverge: sustained operational pressure is not yet matched by a settled forecast of further deterioration.",
